{
  "id": "u02-l03",
  "unit": "u02-pronouns",
  "title": "tui, tumi, apni",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "Bengali has **three** words for 'you', a ladder of closeness:\n\n- **আপনি** (*apni*): respect, for elders, strangers, teachers, shopkeepers.\n- **তুমি** (*tumi*): warmth, for friends, younger people, partners, many parents.\n- **তুই** (*tui*): intimacy, for best friends, younger siblings, small children. Its verb ends in **-িস**: তুই কেমন **আছিস**? Plural: **তোরা**.\n\n**Careful:** তুই to a stranger or an elder is a serious insult. Grandmother → আপনি (some families use তুমি); school best friend → তুই. When in doubt, go one step *more* polite.\n\n**এখানে** = here, **ওখানে** = there. The opposite of আছে is **নেই** (*nei*): রাফি এখানে **নেই** 'Rafi isn't here.'",
  "vocab": [
    {
      "id": "tui",
      "bn": "তুই",
      "tr": "tui",
      "en": "you (intimate)",
      "notes": "For your closest same-age friends, younger siblings, small children. Rude or insulting with strangers and elders."
    },
    {
      "id": "tora",
      "bn": "তোরা",
      "tr": "tora",
      "en": "you (intimate, plural)"
    },
    {
      "id": "achhish",
      "bn": "আছিস",
      "tr": "achhish",
      "en": "are (intimate)",
      "notes": "Goes with তুই / তোরা."
    },
    {
      "id": "ekhane",
      "bn": "এখানে",
      "tr": "ekhane",
      "en": "here"
    },
    {
      "id": "okhane",
      "bn": "ওখানে",
      "tr": "okhane",
      "en": "there"
    },
    {
      "id": "nei",
      "bn": "নেই",
      "tr": "nei",
      "en": "is not (here); there isn't",
      "notes": "Negative of আছ- for every person: আমি নেই, তুমি নেই, সে নেই. Colloquially also নাই (nai)."
    }
  ],
